# ==== WRAPPED HEADERS · the `Block::header_start` discriminators (AXIS H)
#
# ⚠ INPUT→OUTPUT, not a fixpoint — see the `.expected` companion. `gg fmt`
# REJOINS a wrapped header onto one line, so the wrapped spelling exists only
# on pass one; the companion is a fixpoint and the harness's second pass
# asserts that.
#
# WHY THIS AXIS EXISTS. The orphan-pre-close flush decides membership by
# COLUMN: a comment indented past the block's HEADER line was written inside
# the block. Anchoring that test on the block's own `span.start` — the COLON —
# looks right and is wrong: on a wrapped header the colon sits on a
# CONTINUATION line whose indent is at or past the body's, so the test refuses
# everything and the tail comment escapes exactly as before, while its
# single-line sibling is fixed. Hence the typed `Block::header_start` field,
# written at the one indented-suite parser, pointing at the owning construct's
# FIRST line.
#
# THE ROW LIST IS DERIVED FROM A TYPED TABLE, NOT WRITTEN HERE. The authority
# is `BLOCK_PROBES` in `src/parser/tests.rs`: every block-carrying construct has
# a row there carrying a TYPED disposition — `Wrapped`, or
# `NotWrappable(reason)`. Two guards keep it honest, which is why this list is
# now a derivation rather than a claim:
#
#   * `parser_header_start_wiring_census` (tests/lints.rs) censuses the parser's
#     `header_start` wiring sites per enclosing function, so a NEW suite cannot
#     join without showing up and being classified;
#   * `block_probe_dispositions_are_decided` (src/parser/tests.rs) CHECKS each
#     disposition against the source: a `Wrapped` row whose header occupies one
#     line fails, and a `NotWrappable` row whose header already spans lines
#     fails. A false reason cannot survive as prose.
#
# A construct is VALUE-NEEDING iff some LEGAL spelling puts its FIRST line on a
# different line from the position a careless implementation would fall back to.
# There are exactly two such fallbacks, so there are two breaks, and each row
# below names the one that reds its cell:
#
#   BREAK A — `header_start := Block.span.start` (the whole-field fallback).
#   BREAK C — that row's own value := the COLON (the per-row mistake). It is
#     needed because rows 1-4 pass a position that is ALREADY the construct's
#     first line, so BREAK A cannot see them at all.
#
# VALUE-NEEDING — every one has a cell in this file:
#   row 7  fn signature .......................... BREAK A
#   row 6  `if` condition ........................ BREAK A
#   row 6  `elif` condition ...................... BREAK A
#   row 5  `while` condition ..................... BREAK A
#   row 5  `for` iterable ........................ BREAK A
#   row 5  `with … as …` bindings ................ BREAK A
#   row 5  `meta if` condition ................... BREAK A
#   row 5  `meta elif` condition ................. BREAK A
#   row 5  `meta for` range ...................... BREAK A
#   row 5  `meta type f(…)` parameter list ....... BREAK A
#   row 5  `test "…":` name ...................... BREAK A
#   row 5  `bench "…":` name ..................... BREAK A
#   row 9  `meta while` condition ................ BREAK A
#   row 8  `meta match` case expression .......... BREAK A
#   row 1  `catch` / `rethrow` LHS ............... BREAK C
#   row 2  match-STATEMENT arm pattern ........... BREAK C
#   row 2  match-EXPRESSION arm pattern .......... BREAK C
#   row 3  `select` arm operation ................ BREAK C
#   row 4  closure parameter list ................ BREAK C
#
# NON-VALUE-NEEDING — the rule is **a header holding no token that can span
# source lines**, and every one of these is checked by the disposition test:
#   `loop:` · `unsafe:` · `on error:` · a named scope `name:` · `do:` ·
#   `suite setup:` · `suite teardown:` · and EVERY `else:` clause (statement-if,
#   for-else, while-else, match-else, select-else, meta-else, meta-match-else).
# A named scope's identifier is one token AND identifiers cannot span lines —
# both halves are needed, which is the whole lesson below.
#
# ⚠ THIS LIST WAS A SELECTION THREE TIMES, and the third time is why the guards
# exist. It first said row 9 was covered by an unwrapped cell (false after the
# meta-while span hoist). It then said "one cell per value-needing row" while
# nine rows had none. It then excluded `test`/`bench` with the reason "the name
# is a single token, so it cannot wrap" — which conflates ONE TOKEN with ONE
# LINE. Gorget has triple-quoted multi-line strings, so a `test` name spans
# lines; unwiring the `test` row escaped a real comment 4 -> 0 with every gate
# green. "Keyword-only" was the wrong abstraction twice; the rule above is the
# one that survives measurement, and it is now machine-checked rather than
# asserted here.
#
# ⚠ Three authoring traps this file works around: a wrapped bare `if` condition
# is a 10-error parse failure, so the PARENTHESIZED form is used; the wrapped-FN
# cell only discriminates in its ALIGNED-CONTINUATION spelling (a hanging `):`
# at column 0 lets any anchor pass); and the `with` cell wraps a CALL's argument
# list rather than a parenthesized expression, because `gg fmt` deletes grouping
# parens and `with (r + 0) as held:` comes back as `with r + 0 as held:`, which
# does NOT re-parse (10 errors). That is the filed paren-deletion gap, not this
# axis — but it is why this cell is spelled with a call.
#
# RED, observed by BREAK-AND-WATCH on this tree — every row measured, each
# break reverted and the tree confirmed restored-green afterwards. The indents
# are this file's own cells.
#
#   BREAK A · `header_start := Block.span.start`, which reds every row whose
#   value is not already the construct's first line:
#       wrapped-while       8 -> 4      wrapped-if            8 -> 4
#       wrapped-elif        8 -> 4      wrapped-fn            4 -> 0
#       wrapped meta-while  8 -> 4      wrapped meta-elif     8 -> 4
#       wrapped meta-match case  12 -> 8
#       wrapped-for         8 -> 4      wrapped-with          8 -> 4
#       wrapped meta-for    8 -> 4      wrapped meta-if       8 -> 4
#       wrapped meta-type-fn     4 -> 0
#     The UNWRAPPED siblings in `orphan_close_routed_kinds.gg` stay green under
#     this break — which is why they are routed-kind cells, not discriminators.
#
#   BREAK C · one row at a time, seeded from that row's COLON. Rows 1-4 pass a
#   position that is ALREADY the construct's first line, so only this break can
#   see them; each reds ONLY its own cell(s):
#       row 1  catch/rethrow ......... wrapped-catch          8 -> 4
#       row 2  arm bodies ............ wrapped match-arm     12 -> 8
#                                      wrapped match-EXPR arm 12 -> 8
#       row 3  select arms .......... wrapped select-arm     12 -> 8
#       row 4  closures ............. wrapped closure         8 -> 4
#       row 5  `for` iterable ....... wrapped-for             8 -> 4
#       row 5  `with` bindings ...... wrapped-with            8 -> 4
#       row 5  `meta for` range ..... wrapped meta-for        8 -> 4
#       row 5  `meta if` condition .. wrapped meta-if         8 -> 4
#       row 5  `meta type f()` ...... wrapped meta-type-fn    4 -> 0
#       row 6  `elif` (span captured AFTER the condition) —
#                                      wrapped-elif           8 -> 4,
#              while the wrapped-IF tail stays at 8
#       row 8  `meta match` arm ..... wrapped meta-match case 12 -> 8
#       row 9  `meta while` ......... wrapped meta-while      8 -> 4

void wrapped_while(int i):
    while i < 3:
        print(i)
        # tail in a wrapped-while body

void wrapped_if(int i):
    if i < 3:
        print(i)
        # tail in a wrapped-if body
    elif i > 9:
        print(i)
        # tail in a wrapped-elif body

int wrapped_signature(int a_long_parameter_name, int another_long_parameter_name):
    print(a_long_parameter_name)
    return another_long_parameter_name
    # tail in a wrapped-signature body

void wrapped_meta_while[T]():
    meta while bitwidth(T) > 128:
        print("impossible")
        # tail in a wrapped meta-while body

void wrapped_meta_clauses[T]():
    meta if bitwidth(T) > 4096:
        print(1)
    elif bitwidth(T) > 2048:
        print(2)
        # tail in a wrapped meta-elif body

void wrapped_meta_match[T]():
    meta match typename(T):
        case "i" + "nt":
            print(1)
            # tail in a wrapped meta-match case body

void wrapped_for(int n):
    for i in [1, 2]:
        print(i + n)
        # tail in a wrapped-for body

int wrapped_make(int a):
    return a

void wrapped_with(int r):
    with wrapped_make(r) as held:
        print(held)
        # tail in a wrapped-with body

void wrapped_meta_for():
    meta for i in [1, 2]:
        print(i)
        # tail in a wrapped meta-for body

void wrapped_meta_if[T]():
    meta if bitwidth(T) > 4096:
        print(1)
        # tail in a wrapped meta-if body

meta type WrappedMetaTypeFn(Type a_long_parameter_name, Type another_long_parameter_name):
    return a_long_parameter_name
    # tail in a wrapped meta-type-fn body

void wrapped_match_arm(Option[int] x):
    match x:
        case Some(v):
            print(v)
            # tail in a wrapped match-arm body

int wrapped_match_expr_arm(Option[int] x):
    int r = match x:
        case Some(v):
            10
            # tail in a wrapped match-EXPRESSION arm body
        else:
            20
    return r

Chan[int] wrapped_chan():
    return Chan[int](1)

void wrapped_select_arm():
    select:
        case int v = wrapped_chan().recv():
            print(v)
            # tail in a wrapped select-arm body

void wrapped_closure():
    Callable[void(int, int)] g = (int a_long_parameter_name, int another_long_parameter_name):
        print(a_long_parameter_name)
        # tail in a wrapped closure body
    g(1, 2)

test """a wrapped test name, whose closing line is indented
        past the body""":
    print(1)
    # tail in a wrapped test body

bench """a wrapped bench name, whose closing line is indented
        past the body""":
    print(2)
    # tail in a wrapped bench body

int fallible(int x) throws String:
    if x < 0:
        throw "negative"
    return x

int wrapped_catch(int x):
    int a = fallible(x) catch (e):
        print("recovered")
        0
        # tail in a wrapped-catch body
    return a

void main():
    wrapped_while(1)
    wrapped_meta_while[int]()
    wrapped_for(1)
    wrapped_with(1)
    wrapped_meta_for()
    wrapped_meta_if[int]()
    wrapped_match_arm(Some(1))
    print(wrapped_match_expr_arm(Some(1)))
    wrapped_select_arm()
    wrapped_closure()
    wrapped_if(1)
    print(wrapped_signature(1, 2))
    wrapped_meta_clauses[int]()
    wrapped_meta_match[int]()
    print(wrapped_catch(1))
